<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chennakeshava Temple, Somanathapura</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Keshava Temple, Somanathapura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chennakeshava Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somanathapura">Somanathapura</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, is a Vaishnava temple built in the 13th century by Somanatha, a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysala</a> king Narasimha III. Raised on a star-shaped platform with three shrines, it is among the most complete examples of Hoysala architecture. It forms part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-ensembles-of-the-hoysalas">Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas</a> World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chennakeshava-temple-somanathapura.jpg" alt="Chennakeshava Temple, Somanathapura" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Le_temple_de_Chennakesava_(Somanathapura,_Inde)_(14465165685).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
